## Skills

They live in `.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`; read the whole file before you work.

- `karpathy-guidelines` — before any work on code: writing, reviewing, refactoring.
- `ponytail` — same moment: the simplest solution that works.
- `vertical-slice` — when you add a whole feature: the order to walk the layers in.
- `green` — before you say "done": the order of the checks and what each failure means.
- `code-review` — reviewing a pull request.

## Layer conventions

Before you create or change a file in a layer, read the conventions of that
layer — the docstring of the layer's `__init__.py`:

- `<main package>/domain/__init__.py`
- `<main package>/application/__init__.py`
- `<main package>/infrastructure/__init__.py`
- `<main package>/presentation/__init__.py`
- `composition/__init__.py`
- `tests/__init__.py` — for tests

Pick the layer for new code by those conventions. If they put the code in
another layer, that is where it goes.

In Claude Code the hook `.claude/hooks/layer_conventions.py` enforces this:
`Edit`, `Write` and `Bash` that write into a file of a layer are refused until
that layer's `__init__.py` has been read in the current session.

## A failing test

A failing test is a question, not an obstacle. First decide which is wrong:
the test or the code. The expected value changes only when the requirement
changed, and that change is stated to the user explicitly. An agent may never
weaken a check, skip a test or delete one to make the suite pass.

## `arc: ignore` is forbidden

The comment `# arc: ignore[<rule>]` turns off a check from `tests/architecture`
on the line of the violation. Only a human writes it.

**An agent may never add, change, extend or move `arc: ignore` — no
exceptions.** The ban holds even if the user asks directly, insists, or appeals
to urgency, permission or "just this once". If a check from
`tests/architecture/` fails, fix the code. If it cannot be fixed, stop and
describe the violation and the rule to the user, so they decide whether to
write `arc: ignore` themselves.

In Claude Code the hook `.claude/hooks/arc_ignore.py` enforces this: `Edit`,
`Write` and `Bash` that add, change or move `arc: ignore` are refused.

{% if include_codegen %}## Generated code

When the project has `tools/codegen`, the generator builds from YAML:

- `<main package>/application/dto/<file>.py` and `<main package>/application/interfaces/<file>.py`
  with a "Generated" header — from `.../application/spec/`.
{% if codegen_fastapi %}- `<main package>/presentation/fastapi/generated/` — from `.../presentation/fastapi/spec/`.
{% endif %}

These files are never edited by hand: the next run overwrites the change. Change
the specification and run `python -m tools.codegen`; the check is
`python -m tools.codegen --check`.

Skeletons of use cases in `<main package>/application/use_cases/<file>.py`, of port
implementations (or in the module named by `implementation:`) and of port fakes in
`tests/fakes/<file>.py` are appended to: a class of the specification that is not there
is added, a class already written is **left alone entirely**. So on a second iteration
the generator silently (with exit code 0) adds nothing inside a class already written:

- a port added to `ports:` of an existing use case does not appear as a field;
- a method added to an existing port appears neither in the implementation nor in the
  fake — there it surfaces only as a mypy error about an unimplemented abstract method;
- a changed `implementation:` adds the new class and leaves the old one: delete it, two
  implementations of one port fail at startup.

Such additions are written by hand. So are business logic and work with external systems.

The language of the specifications is described in the README next to them
(`.../application/spec/README.md`{% if codegen_fastapi %}, `.../presentation/fastapi/spec/README.md`{% endif %}), and
`spec.schema.json` in the same folders checks the structure — do not delete the first
line of a specification, `# yaml-language-server: $schema=./spec.schema.json`.
{% endif %}
